<h2 id="listing">Step 1: Make your Amazon listing convert</h2>
<p>Every other marketing effort sends people to your book page. If that page doesn’t convince them, you pay for traffic that doesn’t buy. Check these first:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cover:</strong> it must signal the genre instantly and still read as a tiny thumbnail. This is the single biggest factor in whether someone clicks. If yours doesn’t match bestsellers in your category, consider a <a href="/services/book-cover-design-services">professional cover redesign</a> before spending on ads.</li>
  <li><strong>Description:</strong> lead with a hook, not a summary. Use short paragraphs, end with a reason to buy now, and make the first two lines count, because that’s all many shoppers see.</li>
  <li><strong>Categories and keywords:</strong> choose categories where your book can realistically rank, and fill all seven keyword slots with phrases readers actually type.</li>
  <li><strong>Price:</strong> match the norms in your genre. For ebooks, Amazon’s 70% royalty band runs from $2.99 to $12.99 on Amazon.com.</li>
  <li><strong>Look inside:</strong> the first pages must be clean, well formatted and engaging. They’re part of the sales pitch.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-publish-a-book-on-amazon">how to publish a book on Amazon</a> covers keywords, categories and pricing in detail.</p>

<h2 id="platform">Step 2: Build a small author platform you own</h2>
<h3 id="email-list">Start an email list</h3>
<p>An email list is the most dependable marketing tool you’ll have. Social platforms change their algorithms and ad prices rise, but a list of readers who asked to hear from you is yours. Offer something useful for signing up: a bonus chapter, a short story, a checklist or a resource from your nonfiction book. Put the sign-up link on your website, in your social profiles and at the back of your book.</p>
<h3 id="website">Set up an author website and Author Central</h3>
<p>A simple author website gives readers, reviewers and the media one place to find your books, your bio and your sign-up form. Claim your <strong>Amazon Author Central</strong> page too: add a photo, a bio and links between your formats. You also need your books claimed there to run some Amazon Ads. If you’d rather not build a site yourself, our <a href="/services/author-website-design-services">author website design</a> service starts from $600.</p>

<h2 id="reviews">Step 3: Get honest early reviews</h2>
<p>Reviews give shoppers confidence. A book with no reviews is a risk to most readers, so plan for early reviews before launch day.</p>
<ol class="bp-ol">
  <li><strong>Send advance reader copies (ARCs)</strong> four to eight weeks before launch to readers who enjoy your genre, beta readers and people on your email list.</li>
  <li><strong>Make it easy:</strong> send a short reminder with the review link on launch day and again about a week later.</li>
  <li><strong>Follow Amazon’s rules.</strong> Amazon allows free or discounted copies for readers, but you can’t require a review in return or try to influence it, and offering anything else, such as gift cards, can get reviews removed. See <a href="https://kdp.amazon.com/en_US/help/topic/G202101910" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KDP’s customer review guidelines</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Never buy or swap reviews.</strong> Amazon removes them and can take action against your account.</li>
</ol>
<div class="bp-tip"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> add a short, polite note at the end of your book asking readers to leave an honest review. It costs nothing and works for every copy you sell, long after launch.</p></div>

<h2 id="launch">Step 4: Plan a focused launch week</h2>
<figure class="bp-fig"><img src="/assets/img/lp/dashboard.webp" alt="A self-publishing dashboard showing a book’s sales during launch week" width="1254" height="1254" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A concentrated launch week helps Amazon learn who your book is for, which supports visibility afterwards.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Concentrating sales and attention into a few days helps your book get noticed in its categories. A practical launch week looks like this:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> email your list, post on your channels and tell friends, colleagues and communities who’d genuinely be interested.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 2–4:</strong> run a launch price or promotion and, if it fits your budget, book a slot on an established ebook promotion site.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 3–7:</strong> start small Amazon Ads campaigns, follow up with advance readers about reviews, and share early reader reactions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pre-orders help too: they let you share a link before launch and turn early interest into sales from day one.</p>

<h2 id="amazon-ads">Step 5: Use Amazon Ads, carefully</h2>
<p>Amazon Ads put your book in front of shoppers who are already searching for books like yours, and you only pay when someone clicks. <a href="https://advertising.amazon.com/library/guides/advertising-books-on-amazon-authors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon’s guide for authors</a> covers the basics, and its FAQ lists a minimum budget of $1 a day, with around $10 a day suggested to keep ads running all day.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Start with Sponsored Products</strong> using automatic targeting plus a manual campaign with specific keywords and comparable authors.</li>
  <li><strong>Set a daily budget you’re comfortable losing</strong> while you learn, and review results weekly, not hourly.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch clicks and orders, not just impressions.</strong> If a keyword gets clicks but no sales, the listing (or the targeting) is the problem.</li>
  <li><strong>Cut what doesn’t work</strong> and move budget to the keywords and placements that sell.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ads work best with a series or several books, because one reader can buy more than one title. With a single book, keep spending tight until you know your numbers.</p>

